#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c32fc9e-ce9a-535b-a8b3-06c486c6cd67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486898} {:answer/id #uuid "a059a8b3-f1ed-5de8-a334-40e4ba40f83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.602" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486959} {:answer/id #uuid "a6776cbd-ebfc-53b8-ad96-0550911cd2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.688" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486972} {:answer/id #uuid "ec76cd0f-4509-5d07-9c06-06e62501a6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.672" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487097} {:answer/id #uuid "efaec769-99d0-583b-aad4-773115f51b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.668" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c32fc9e-ce9a-535b-a8b3-06c486c6cd67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486898} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e23228-7c0d-5766-b1ab-6e8ce201deb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a806821e-50df-52c7-a557-34976fdac21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-148079" :question/problem "An English teacher believes that students who prefer cursive handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove his belief, he puts some students into groups of three according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in cursive.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades, and $12%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $15%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $17%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\nIf $56%$ of students are female, and $16%$ of female students write in cursive, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that the student writes in cursive, and $L,A,H$ be the events that the student has low, average, or high grades, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 &  & P(W|L)=0.12 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.15 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.56.\n$$\n\n- Since $16%$ of female students write in cursive, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.16.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female, which is represented as $P(F|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.16)(0.56)}{(0.2)(0.12)+(0.65)(0.15)+(0.15)(0.17)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0896}{0.147} \\\\  & ≈0.610,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ee0324-47ce-520c-be1b-617fc28bde99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486628} {:answer/id #uuid "1f964113-d9c4-53ce-a730-891983a25308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486654} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd21a5c-17af-557a-936c-d9cb120f8e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486762} {:answer/id #uuid "56ac6ed1-d21c-50b6-90e0-44a21cfc1766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486783} {:answer/id #uuid "8146efe8-c4c6-5ac7-a7c5-ddfd418eda3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd21a5c-17af-557a-936c-d9cb120f8e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486762} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75bc298-c38f-59f1-b1e6-e063f2062f22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f697c855-b9dd-5b7e-bb24-b23154c897b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-148085" :question/problem "A gambling room has three roulette tables, table $1,$ table $2,$ and table $3.$ Data of all bets of a particular evening are collected.\n\n- $30%$ of bets were played at table $1,$ and $5%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $2,$ and $6%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $3,$ and $7%$ of those bets were won.\n\nIf $10%$ of bets were played in the first hour, and $4%$ of bets made in the first hour were won, what is the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet was played in the first hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a bet is played in the first hour, $W$ be the event that a bet is won, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a bet is played at table $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{T}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{3})=0.07\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.35+0.35=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of bets in the entire evening are played in the first hour, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of bets made in the first hour are won, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.04.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet is played in the first hour, which is represented as $P(F|W).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(W|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(W|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.1)}{(0.05)(0.3)+(0.06)(0.35)+(0.07)(0.35)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0605} \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}]}
